    "**Reading Plot C — takeaways.** - to review\n",
    "\n",
    "- **The bounding is what matters, not the flavour.** `MPNN3 316+RS` (RobustScaler) is the clear loser —\n",
    "  its CV-fold Pearson r drops to, and on HLM/RLM/SOL *below*, graph-only `MPNN1`. Feeding a neural net\n",
    "  unbounded descriptors is worse than giving it none. Swap to a bounded transform and it jumps into the top\n",
    "  tier: `MPNN3 (QT)`, `MPNN4 (raw-200 + QT)` and `MPNN2 (rdkit_2d_normalized, paper-faithful)` are all\n",
    "  effectively tied — the per-endpoint \"winner\" flips between them and between CV and test, which is the\n",
    "  signature of no real difference.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Paper-faithful is enough.** The two descriptor blocks are *different sets, not nested*: MPNN3's rmoldes\n",
    "  is **50 descriptors that expand to 316 features** (44 scalar + 6 vector, e.g. AUTOCORR2D → 192), while\n",
    "  MPNN2/MPNN4 use descriptastorus's **200 descriptors = 200 features**. MPNN3 (316-feature input) and MPNN2\n",
    "  (200-feature input) tie — so neither the larger input dimensionality nor the specific descriptor set is the\n",
    "  lever; a reasonable *bounded* block is. And since MPNN2 is the paper's own `rdkit_2d_normalized`, there's no\n",
    "  need for the empirical-QT variants (`MPNN3/MPNN4`) — they're here only to *show* the flavour doesn't matter.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Why the same fix rescues MPNN3 but not FCNN.** For MPNN, the descriptor block is fed into the readout\n",
    "  head *alongside the learned graph embedding*, and ChemProp keeps external features unscaled\n",
    "  (`--no_features_scaling`). An unbounded RobustScaler'd block then sits on a completely different scale\n",
    "  from the embedding, and that mismatch destabilises the head — bounding the descriptors to [0, 1] removes\n",
    "  it. FCNN sees the descriptors *alone*: there's no embedding to clash with, so RobustScaler is already\n",
    "  adequate, and QuantileTransformer's rank-flattening (which throws away magnitude spacing) buys nothing —\n",
    "  hence FCNN2 ≈ or slightly worse than FCNN. The bounded-input rule is real but *conditional* on that mix.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Rigor / next step (optional).** The claims above — \"RobustScaler is significantly worse\" and \"the QT\n",
    "  variants are tied\" — are eyeball-plus-gap, not tested. The §5.5 machinery (`AnovaRM` + Tukey HSD on the 15\n",
    "  CV-fold scores) applies directly to these five MPNN variants and is cheap to reuse; it would likely\n",
    "  confirm RS as a significant drop and `MPNN3/3/4` as not-significantly-different. Worth doing if these\n",
    "  normalization claims go in the report. One caveat for any such test: CV folds share training data, so the scores aren't independent and naive p-values (t-test/Tukey/AnovaRM alike) run optimistic — the rigorous form is a corrected resampled *t* (Nadeau–Bengio). It wouldn't change the verdict here, though: the RS→QT effect is far too large to lose significance and the MPNN2–MPNN4 gap far too small to gain it.\n"
