   "source": [
    "**Why the significance colours shift when `HEATMAP_MODEL_LIST` changes.** Each heatmap cell is one model pair at one endpoint, but its `p-adj` is *not* computed in isolation — `pairwise_tukeyhsd` fits a single one-way model over **every** model in the call, so adding models (e.g. FCNN/MPNN alongside RF/SVM/XGBoost/LightGBM) moves the colour of pairs that were already there, even though those models' scores never changed. Two mechanisms, both tied to the number of models `k`:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Family-wise multiple-comparison correction.** Tukey controls the error rate across all `C(k,2)` pairwise comparisons at once. More models → more comparisons → the studentized-range critical value `q(k, df)` rises → every `p-adj` gets inflated (larger). This alone can push a borderline pair from p<0.05 to NS, or p<0.001 down to p<0.01.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. **Pooled within-group variance.** The standard error of any pair's difference is `sqrt(MSE_within · (1/n₁ + 1/n₂))`, where `MSE_within` is pooled across **all** models in the call. Add noisier models (MPNNs have wider fold-to-fold scatter) and that pooled variance grows, inflating `p-adj` further. The residual df `N − k` also shifts.\n",
    "\n",
    "Both mechanisms push the same way: adding models generally makes it **harder** for any given pair to stay significant. The one thing that does **not** change is the `meandiff` itself (the top number in each cell) — it's genuinely pairwise (`μ_row − μ_col`) and stable. Only its significance tier moves. That stable `meandiff` with a shifting colour is the tell that this is the Tukey family-wide correction + pooled variance at work, not a change in the underlying model performance.\n",
    "\n",
    "Note the plain `pairwise_tukeyhsd` here treats all 15 folds × k models as independent one-way observations — it does **not** use the fold-pairing that the `AnovaRM` omnibus test does (statsmodels has no repeated-measures Tukey), which is why the pooled-variance effect isn't offset by pairing."
   ]
